# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-

def blocks():
    return [
        {"t": "h1", "text": "Interview tips"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Indian loops for this level follow a familiar path. The words change by company. The decisions do not. Clear each round by showing the next person that you can do the job they are screening for. Technical depth is practiced in the 10-day roadmap, the deep dive, and the DSA and system-design guide. This section is how you carry that work into the process."},
        {"t": "table", "headers": ["Round", "What they decide", "What you prepare"], "rows": [
            ["Recruiter screen", "Title, stack, city, notice, and whether pay can meet.", "Floor, notice, positioning line, and one proof metric."],
            ["Hiring manager", "Scope, judgment, and whether they can work with you.", "One project deep dive and a 90-day sketch."],
            ["Coding or practical .NET", "Whether you can still build.", "Roadmap Days 1, 3, 4, and 5. Speak while you type."],
            ["System design", "Boundaries, data, failure, and rollback.", "DSA and system-design guide, plus roadmap Days 7 and 9."],
            ["Leadership", "A real decision and how the team works now.", "Five stories from your own career, in the roadmap's story bank."],
            ["HR or offer", "Joining date, location, and the letter.", "The same CTC numbers as the first screen."],
        ]},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Before every screen, write three lines on paper: the company type, why you want this role, and the one project you will tell. Score practice answers the way the roadmap says. A strong answer is correct, names a tradeoff, and names a consequence for a user, an on-call person, a cost, or a teammate."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Tell me about yourself"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Ninety seconds. The positioning line, then one proof sentence, then the role you want. Stop."},
        {"t": "code", "text": "I have eight years building .NET backends. I am moving into a tech lead role where I still own Web API design, EF Core, SQL, and Azure, and I also own the technical plan: service boundaries, migration risk, code review, and a safe release.\nIn the last system I owned, we cut p95 on the order API from 800 ms to 220 ms with one SQL query and a covering index.\nI am looking for that scope on a product team in Bengaluru. My notice is 30 days."},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Replace the middle sentence with your system and your number. Practice it out loud until it fits in ninety seconds."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Current CTC, expected CTC, and notice"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Every Indian screen asks these. Answer with the breakup. The numbers match the resume, Naukri, and the documents you can show later."},
        {"t": "lead", "title": "Current pay", "text": "My fixed is A lakh. Variable is B, and last year it paid W. The CTC on my letter is T. I can walk through that on a slip if we reach an offer."},
        {"t": "lead", "title": "Expected pay", "text": "For a tech lead seat I compare offers on fixed cash first. I am targeting fixed around F, and a total that can land near N once variable and equity are clear. If you have a band for this role, tell me the band and I will say whether it fits."},
        {"t": "lead", "title": "Notice", "text": "My contract notice is N days. I can start the process now and join on date D. I will confirm any early release only after this offer is written."},
        {"t": "p", "text": "If they demand a single expected number and will not share a band, give your target fixed and say the total depends on variable and equity. Keep that number at or above your floor. A thirty-day notice is a hiring advantage. If your contract says ninety days, quote ninety. Add a buyout or an early release only when you have done it before or your manager has already agreed. A promised date you cannot hit shows up when the background check asks your current HR for the last working day."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "The rest of the screen"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "Why are you looking: the scope you want next. Tech lead ownership of the API, the data, Azure, and the release plan.",
            "Why this company: one fact about their product, one line from the job description, and the part of that scope you have already done.",
            "Other processes: name the stage you are in. Cite another offer only when you can forward the letter.",
            "A weakness: one real gap and the practice you do now. For example, you used to jump to a new service. You now write the data owner and the failure mode first, and you keep one deployable until those are clear.",
        ]},
        {"t": "p", "text": "The reason you give for looking is the work you want. A complaint about a manager becomes the story they remember. Save the detail for a private decision about whether you join."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Hiring manager"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "They already believe you know C#. They are testing judgment. Walk one project from the story bank: the constraint, the decision, the metric, and what the team does now. When they ask what you would do in ninety days, say you will learn the production pain from the on-call notes and the last incidents, ship one improvement the team already wants, and write the review or release check that would have caught it. That sketch shows you can lead without pretending you know their system on day one."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Coding, design, and leadership"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "On a coding round, ask what the input size is and what should happen on bad input. Narrate the approach, then type. Days 1, 3, 4, and 5 of the roadmap cover the C#, ASP.NET Core, EF Core, and SQL they still test. The DSA guide covers the structures. If you go blank, say what you would look up and which part you do know. Invented API limits and invented Azure prices lose the room. The roadmap already says to name the document or the metric you would check."},
        {"t": "p", "text": "On a design round, draw one boundary, the data owner, the failure, and the rollback. Days 7 and 9, and the system-design guide, are the practice. Stop adding services when one deployable still fits."},
        {"t": "p", "text": "On a leadership round, use your five stories: a production incident and the minute you chose rollback or fix-forward, a slow endpoint and the number after the fix, a migration and what you refused to rewrite, a design you pushed back on, and a person who now owns something you used to own. Each story needs your decision and a metric."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Questions you ask them"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Three questions are enough. Pick from this list based on what they did not already answer."},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "Who owns this service in production, and what does the on-call week look like?",
            "What is the deploy path, and how do you roll back?",
            "Why is the role open, and what should I own in the first ninety days?",
            "How large is the team, and which decisions does the tech lead make without a committee?",
            "What does a strong first year in this seat look like?",
        ]},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "The day of the interview"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "A quiet room, a charged laptop, and the IDE open on an empty console project.",
            "A SQL tool ready if they said the round includes the database. Paper for a design. Water. Phone on silent.",
            "Join five minutes early. If you need a moment to think, say you are taking a moment, then start with the decision or the definition.",
            "Write down every question you missed as soon as you hang up. That list is the next practice hour.",
        ]},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "After the round"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Send a short note the same day to the recruiter or the person who arranged the call. One specific point from the conversation is enough."},
        {"t": "code", "text": "Thank you for today's conversation about the order API and the release process. I enjoyed the discussion of how the team rolls back. I am glad to continue, and my notice is 30 days."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Take-home work"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "A time-boxed exercise of a few hours, reviewed by the hiring manager, is a normal part of some loops. Ask how long they expect and who marks it. A multi-day build that matches their live product, or unpaid work they could ship to a client, is outside a hiring exercise. Decline that and offer a design discussion or a shorter sample instead."},
        {"t": "warn", "title": "Stop the process", "text": "Walk away from a joining bond, a training fee, salary offered in cash outside the letter, a recruiter who will not name the client, or anyone who asks you to resign before a written offer. A stack that changes completely after you send documents is a different job. Put it back through the five-minute check."},
        {"t": "break"},
        {"t": "h1", "text": "The offer"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Compare fixed cash first. Then variable and the condition that pays it. Then joining bonus and the clawback. Then equity: grant, vesting, cliff, strike, and whether anyone has sold. Then provident fund, insurance, probation length, and the notice period after you join. A verbal number is a conversation. The letter is the offer."},
        {"t": "table", "headers": ["Line", "Offer A", "Offer B"], "rows": [
            ["Fixed pay", "", ""],
            ["Variable and when it paid last year", "", ""],
            ["Joining bonus and clawback", "", ""],
            ["Equity: vesting, cliff, strike, sale path", "", ""],
            ["Provident fund, gratuity, insurance", "", ""],
            ["Probation and notice after joining", "", ""],
            ["City and weekly office days", "", ""],
            ["Joining date they will accept", "", ""],
        ]},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Accept in writing after the letter matches what you agreed. Resign after that written acceptance. Serve the notice your current contract requires. If they run background verification, the titles, dates, and CTC you quoted should match the papers. Complete the checks they schedule. Tell your current manager when you are ready to serve notice, with the joining date already backed by the new letter."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "How to ask for a change"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "A competing written offer is the lever that moves fixed pay. Mail one clear ask. Get the change on the revised letter. A chat message is not the annexure."},
        {"t": "code", "text": "Thank you for the offer. I want to join this team. To sign, I need fixed pay of F lakh, or a joining bonus of J that closes the gap with the other written offer I am holding. I can sign by Friday if the revised letter reflects that."},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Send that only when the other offer is real and you would show it. If they cannot move fixed pay, ask for a joining bonus or a written review date, and decide against your floor. Quote the same current CTC you gave in the first screen."},
        {"t": "break"},
        {"t": "h1", "text": "Thirty days"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Run this once. If a screen arrives in week one, pull the interview practice forward and keep the daily applications smaller that day. The roadmap's practice blocks are 3.5 to 4 hours. Search days stay near an hour so you still have voice left for the technical work."},
        {"t": "table", "headers": ["Week", "Focus", "Done when"], "rows": [
            ["1", "Resume, LinkedIn, Naukri, and either Instahyre or Cutshort. Write the floor. List about 25 companies. Turn on alerts.", "The three profiles tell the same story, and the tracker has its header row."],
            ["2", "Five tailored applications each weekday. Ten recruiter or hiring-manager notes. Five referral asks with a job link. Log every conversation.", "Every target-company application has a source and a next action."],
            ["3", "Recruiter screens with the CTC script. Ninety-second answers from the roadmap. One design spoken from the system-design guide. One story from your own project each day.", "You can give current pay, expected pay, and notice without looking down, and one project without wandering."],
            ["4", "Hiring-manager and panel rounds. Fill the offer table when letters arrive. Ask for the change you need. Resign after the written acceptance.", "The tracker shows either a signed letter or a dated next round."],
        ]},
        {"t": "note", "title": "If the month ends without an offer", "text": "Count conversations, not clicks. No replies means the headline, the cities, or the company list needs a rewrite. Replies under the floor means the salary filter and the services pile need a cut. Screens that stall on design or on stories mean the next month is the roadmap and the system-design guide, spoken, while the five applications a day continue. The search keeps running while you practice."},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Keep the floor written on the tracker. The high-paying offer is the one that clears it on fixed cash, in a team whose stack you already named out loud, with a letter you can sign."},
    ]
